¶
'''tee<sup>1</sup>†''', ''v.'' [ME. ''teen'', ''ten'' (without inf. ending ''tee'', ''te'') (pret. ''tigh'', ''teiȝ'', ''teȝ'', ''teh'', pl. ''tuwen'', ''tuȝen'', ''tuhen'', pp. ''towen'', ''toȝen''), < AS. ''teón'', ''tión'' (pret. ''teáh'', pl. ''tugon'', pp. ''togen'') = OS. ''tiohan'', ''tion'', ''tian'' = OFries. ''tīa'' = MLG. ''tien'', ''tēn'', LG. ''teën'' = OHG. ''ziohan'', MHG. G. ''ziehen'' = Icel. *''tjūga'' (in pp. ''toginn'') = Goth. ''tiuhan'', draw, lead, = L. ''ducere'', draw, lead: see ''duct'', ''adduce'', ''conduce'', ''educe'', etc. This obs. verb is represented in mod. E. by the derived ''tow''<sup>1</sup>, ''tug'', ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>; the pp. exists unrecognized in the second element of ''wanton''. Hence also ult. ''team'', ''teem''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To draw; lead.
{{smaller block|A thousend men ne mowe hire enes of the stede ''teo''.
{{right|''Early Eng. Poems'' (ed. Furnivall), xxi. 112. (''Stratmann.'')}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To draw away; go; proceed.
{{smaller block|<poem>I wyl me sum other waye, that he ne wayte after;
I schal ''tee'' in-to Tarce, & tary there a whyle.</poem>
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), iii. 87.}}}}

'''tee<sup>2</sup>''' (tē), ''v.'' A dialectal form of ''tie''<sup>1</sup>.

'''tee<sup>3</sup>''' (tē), ''n.'' [Perhaps ult. < Icel. ''tjā'', point out, akin to AS. ''tǣcan'', point out, teach: see ''teach''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A mark toward which missiles, as balls, quoits, or curling-stones, are aimed in different games.
{{smaller block|Just outside there is a trimly kept bowling-green, in which the club members practise the gentle art of reaching the ''tee'' when the waning afternoon releases them from their desk or counter. {{right|''W. Black'', In Far Lochaber, ii.}}}}
'''2.''' In the game of golf, the sand or earth on which the ball is very slightly raised at the beginning of play for each hole. See the quotation under ''tee''<sup>3</sup>, ''v.''
